  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1258 PM MDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nWater Vapor and upper air models show southwest flow aloft, as\nupper high sits over the southeastern CONUS and upper low sits\nover northern CA. Weak shortwave currently sits over eastern WY.\n18Z surface analysis shows surface low over the ND/MT/SD border\nwith a frontal boundary stretching southwest into WY. Winds are\ncurrently breezy out of the south to southeast over most of the\nforecast area, while northwest winds are ongoing for parts of\nnortheast WY that are north of the boundary. Current temps range\nfrom the mid 60s to mid 70s this afternoon. \n\nLingering cloud cover will likely temper highs a bit today from\nthe previous forecast, otherwise no significant weather expected\nthe rest of the day. Attention then turns to Thursday, as a\nshortwave moves northeast from CO through WY and into western SD.\nWith ample moisture from low level southeasterly flow,\nprecipitation is expected to move into northeast WY late Thursday\nmorning, and expand into SD throughout the day. HREF 24 hour\nprobabilities for 0.5\" or more QPF range from 50-90% for much of\nwestern SD, mainly everywhere south of hwy 212. Precipitation is\nexpected to move out overnight into Friday morning. \n\nFriday will be mostly dry as shortwave ridging sets up over the\nnorthern plains, but by Saturday the aformentioned upper low\nshifts over the Rockies bringing cooler temperatures and more \npotential for widespread precipitation Saturday and Sunday. Severe\nweather is not expected with either of these disturbances. \n\nEarly next week an upper high sets up over the southern plains,\nand ridging over the central/northern plains will bring a gradual\nwarming trend back to above normal temperatures by Tuesday.",
